An agent regression suite should represent the ways the system actually fails: a wrong tool, a missing citation, an unsafe action, a retry loop, a timeout, or a result that is technically valid but useless. Start with reviewed production traces, redact sensitive values, and preserve the reason each case matters.

## Store the decision context

Each case needs an input class, expected outcome, allowed tools, relevant evidence, evaluator version, and failure label. Keep the original trace reference when possible so a test remains connected to the execution that inspired it.

The [testing path from unit tests to production evals](/blog/ai-agent-testing-unit-tests-production-evals) explains the layers; the [evaluation guide](/blog/ai-agent-evaluation-practical-guide) covers scoring and review. Regression testing is the bridge that makes those layers learn from operation.

```yaml
id: support-refund-ambiguous-date
expectedStatus: ask_user
forbiddenTools: [issue_refund]
checks:
  - no_irreversible_action
  - asks_for_missing_date
```

## Compare behavior, not only scores

Run the baseline and candidate on the same cases. Compare terminal status, tool sequence, policy decisions, latency, cost, and evaluator explanations. A candidate that keeps the final answer but changes a risky intermediate action deserves review.

## Keep the suite alive

Add a case after every confirmed incident, remove duplicates, and periodically sample successful runs for coverage gaps. Tag cases by workflow, model, release, and failure mechanism. A smaller suite with clear ownership is more useful than a large folder no one trusts.
